WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.405 --> 00:00:10.464
- [Narrator] If you want
to make real progress,

00:00:10.464 --> 00:00:12.341
you have to challenge yourself to rethink

00:00:12.341 --> 00:00:14.758
what you consider impossible.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.656
The expression "like talking to a wall"

00:00:18.656 --> 00:00:20.832
means that the person you're talking to

00:00:20.832 --> 00:00:25.568
can't or won't listen
because walls can't listen.

00:00:25.568 --> 00:00:26.568
Or can they?

00:00:27.883 --> 00:00:30.112
It's exactly this kind of accepted wisdom

00:00:30.112 --> 00:00:32.235
that we love debunking.

00:00:32.235 --> 00:00:34.720
If you have ingenuity and determination,

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.470
then really nothing's impossible.

00:00:39.115 --> 00:00:41.056
We're going to be
conducting our experiment

00:00:41.056 --> 00:00:43.019
at the Berlin Wall Memorial.

00:00:43.019 --> 00:00:44.587
It's the only section where both sides

00:00:44.587 --> 00:00:46.475
of the wall are still standing

00:00:46.475 --> 00:00:48.853
with no man's land in between.

00:00:48.853 --> 00:00:50.933
To get the wall to
listen, we're going to use

00:00:50.933 --> 00:00:53.400
something called an accelerometer.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.661
We normally use these accelerometers

00:00:55.661 --> 00:00:57.154
to pick up microscopic vibrations

00:00:57.154 --> 00:01:00.557
inside machines like
turbines and jet engines.

00:01:00.557 --> 00:01:02.552
They help us diagnose potential problems

00:01:02.552 --> 00:01:04.802
before they arise so that we can prevent

00:01:04.802 --> 00:01:07.469
power outages and flight delays.

00:01:08.877 --> 00:01:10.541
We're going to attach an accelerometer

00:01:10.541 --> 00:01:12.450
to one side of the wall.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:14.765
If we're right, it should
be sensitive enough

00:01:14.765 --> 00:01:16.845
to detect the tiny vibrations produced

00:01:16.845 --> 00:01:20.088
by a person talking on the other side.

00:01:20.088 --> 00:01:21.688
We'll be filtering the signal,

00:01:21.688 --> 00:01:23.117
and then playing it through speakers

00:01:23.117 --> 00:01:26.034
to a group of kids on the far side.

00:01:30.402 --> 00:01:33.048
So, in a way, the kids will be listening

00:01:33.048 --> 00:01:35.381
to what the wall is hearing.

00:01:36.493 --> 00:01:39.074
We won't have someone
just talking to a wall,

00:01:39.074 --> 00:01:41.991
he'll be reading a whole storybook.

00:01:44.589 --> 00:01:46.422
Let's see if it works.

00:01:58.018 --> 00:01:59.435
- The Cat's Tale.

00:02:03.053 --> 00:02:04.303
The Cat's Tale.

00:02:05.346 --> 00:02:09.048
In a land far away but also quite near,

00:02:09.048 --> 00:02:11.544
there were two groups of cats.

00:02:11.544 --> 00:02:13.496
Oh didn't you hear?

00:02:13.496 --> 00:02:15.074
Did I say they were the same?

00:02:15.074 --> 00:02:16.738
No, Not quite.

00:02:16.738 --> 00:02:20.205
There was one difference
although very slight.

00:02:20.205 --> 00:02:23.146
For now let me tell you
just how it will end.

00:02:23.146 --> 00:02:26.815
(wall muffles the speaker)

00:02:26.815 --> 00:02:30.898
And lived forever in peace
just as they ought to.

00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:37.771
(everyone claps)

